course: language
lesson: 26
title: Phrasal verbs: get up, give up, make up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A phrasal verb is a verb plus a small word like up, out, on, in or off, and together they make a new meaning that you often can't guess from the parts.
explain: On a school morning Mum shouts "Get up!", then "Get out of the bathroom!", then "Get on with your breakfast!"; three "gets", three different jobs.
explain: The small word (the particle) changes everything: "give up" means stop trying, but "give in" your homework means hand it in.
explain: One phrasal verb can have several meanings, so the situation decides: Dad "picking you up" at 5 means collecting you in the car, not lifting you into the air.
explain: Phrasal verbs are some of the hardest bits of English for learners, so be patient when a friend gets them muddled.
fact: A phrasal verb is a verb plus a small word called a particle (up, out, on, in, off, away, down), making a new meaning.
fact: "Get up" means rise from bed, "get out" means leave, and "get on with" means carry on doing something.
fact: "Give up" means stop trying; "give in" means hand in, and it can also mean surrender.
fact: "Pick up" can mean lift, collect someone, learn something easily ("she picked up French") or answer the phone.
fact: "Make up" can mean invent a story, become friends again after a fight, or put on make-up; "make up your mind" means decide.
fact: "Spill the tea" is slang from African American speech, now used by many young people, meaning "share the gossip"; no real tea is spilt.
fact: "Warm up" means do gentle exercise before sport, and "cool down" means slow down gently afterwards.
fact: "Put away" means tidy into place, "put on" means wear, "put off" means delay or discourage, and "put up with" means put up with something you don't like, that is, tolerate it.
fact: "Chill out" (slang), "simmer down" (an older phrase from cooking) and "calm down" (standard) all mean become calm.
fact: "Look up" means find information; with "it" or "them" the particle goes after: "look it up", not "look up it".
fact: "Sort out" means organise or fix; in British slang "sorted" means done or all fine, and a "tip" is a very messy place.
exercise: Mum says "Get on with it!" What does she want? | Carry on with what you are doing | Climb up on top of the kitchen table | Get off the bus | Stop doing it
exercise: Pip hears "get up", "get out" and "get on with it". Why do they mean different things? | The small word changes the meaning | They all mean exactly the same thing, just said louder | Mum is mixing up her words | "Get" has no meaning at all
exercise: Ivy's friend says "Don't give up!" What does it mean? | Don't stop trying | Don't hand it in | Don't give presents | Don't look up
exercise: Teacher says "Give your homework in!" What should Ivy do? | Hand it to the teacher | Stop doing it | Throw it away | Give it to a friend
exercise: Dad says "I'll pick you up at 5." What will he do? | Collect Tom in the car | Lift Tom into the air | Phone Tom | Teach Tom something
exercise: Which sentence uses "pick up" to mean learn easily? | She picked up French on holiday | He picked up the pencil from the floor | Dad picked me up from school at three | Gran picked up the phone
exercise: Teacher says "Make up your mind." What does she want? | Decide | Invent a story | Put on make-up | Become friends again
exercise: After a fight, two friends "make up". What happened? | They became friends again | They invented a lie | They put on face paint | They went to bed
exercise: A girl from Leeds says "Spill the tea, then." What does she want? | The gossip, the full story | A cup of tea poured on the floor | Someone to clean up | A drink of tea
exercise: The football coach shouts "Cool down!" after the match. What should the team do? | Slow down gently after exercise | Drink lots of ice-cold water straight away | Sit in a fridge | Go home straight away
exercise: Mum says "Don't put me off!" What is Charlie doing? | Distracting her from what she's doing | Helping her put her coat on before work | Tidying away toys | Switching off the light in her room
exercise: What does "put up with" mean? | Tolerate | Build | Hang on a wall | Tidy away
exercise: Theo's mate says "Chill out, bro." What does he mean? | Calm down | Go outside in the cold | Eat some ice cream | Open the fridge
exercise: Marta says "I looked the word." What's the right way? | I looked it up | I looked up it | I looked it | I up-looked it
exercise: What does "sort it out" mean? | Organise or fix it | Throw it all away | Put it in a sorting machine | Count it
exercise: Leo says "Sorted, innit." What does "sorted" mean in British slang? | Done, all fine | Messy | Ready to start | Sorted by colour
test: Your mate goes "phrasal verbs are easy, any learner can guess 'em, bruv." What's true? | They're among the hardest bits of English for learners | He's right, you can always guess them from the main verb | Learners never use them | They only exist in America
test: Freddie says "make up" always means put on make-up. What's true? | It can mean invent, become friends again, or put on make-up | He's right, it has only one meaning, and that's putting on make-up | It only means become friends | It only means decide
test: What would happen if you told an English learner "I'll pick you up" at the airport, and they didn't know the phrasal verb? | They might think you'll lift them in the air | They'd understand perfectly | They'd think you would phone them from the car park | They'd think you were leaving
test: Lucy asks if the football team needs thermometers for "warm up". What's the answer? | No, it means gentle exercise to get ready | Yes, they must be warm to play | Yes, it's a medical test the coach does before kick-off | No, it means put on a coat
test: Your nan says "simmer down, pet" and your mate says "chill out". ("Pet" is a North East word for dear.) What's the difference? | Same meaning, one older and one newer slang | Nan wants you to cook | Your mate wants you to go outside | Nan is angry and your mate is not
test: A cartoon shows Tom's dad literally lifting him into the car at 5 o'clock. What did "pick you up" really mean? | Collect in the car | Lift into the air | Phone at 5 | Pick him like a flower
test: Your older cousin says "y'all better put on your coats, it's wicked cold." ("Wicked" in New England means very.) What should you do? | Wear your coats | Tidy your coats away | Delay putting on coats | Throw your coats out
test: Your teacher says "Stop putting it off and start your project!" What have you been doing? | Delaying it | Wearing it | Tidying it | Finishing it early
test: Your bro texts "room's a tip, mum's fuming, sort it out or ur dead lol." What does he mean? | Your room is very messy; tidy it before Mum gets cross | Your room has a real rubbish dump inside it, so Mum is calling the council | Mum is giving you money | Your room needs a new tip jar
test: Marta says "Can you look up it for me?" How can you help her kindly? | Tell her "look it up" goes with the "it" in the middle | Laugh and walk away | Tell her "look up it" is perfect, because English has no rules for this | Tell her she can't learn English
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a phrasal verb? | A verb combined with a particle so as to produce a distinct meaning | A verb that is spelled identically in the present and the past tense, such as "put" | A verb borrowed from another language | A verb used only in written English
exam: Identify the meaning of the expression "to give up" as distinguished from "to give in" (in the sense of homework). | To cease attempting, as opposed to to submit | To submit, as opposed to to cease attempting | Both expressions are identical in meaning | Both expressions signify generosity
exam: Select the grammatically acceptable construction employing the pronoun "it" with the verb "look up". | Look it up | Look up it | Look it upward | Up look it
exam: Ascertain the meaning of "to put up with" in standard English usage. | To tolerate | To construct | To store | To postpone
